  "response": "Lawrence Summers made his remarks on women in science in January 2005. The occasion was a National Bureau of Economic Research conference on diversifying the science and engineering workforce, a meeting of economists and policy analysts convened to examine why the scientific labor force remained so unevenly composed. The remarks provoked an immediate public controversy. More durably, they set an empirical agenda: they raised questions about aptitude, preference, and bias that later investigators would examine with large datasets and controlled experiments.\n\nSummers gave his 2005 remarks at a conference hosted by MIT. Partway through that address, he went on to suggest that greater male variability at the high end of aptitude distributions helps explain women's underrepresentation. That proposition concerns the tails of statistical distributions rather than their centers, and it was this feature that later research would test most directly. Whereas his argument located the problem in the distribution of ability, the institutional consequences fell on Summers himself. Summers was still serving as Harvard's president at the end of 2005. The record on this point admits only two possibilities: either he remained in office at the end of 2005, or he had left the Harvard presidency before the end of 2005. The two cannot both be true, and one of them must hold.\n\nThe first major empirical test concerned average performance. Hyde and colleagues found no meaningful gender difference in the average math performance of US students in grades 2 through 11. In other words, according to Hyde and colleagues' 2008 analysis, US girls and boys in grades 2 through 11 performed essentially equally in mathematics on average. That parity stands even though Hyde and colleagues found in 2008 that boys substantially outperformed girls on US state mathematics assessments. The average-performance results therefore offered little support for any account resting on a general deficit in female mathematical ability. They left open only the narrower question of variance at the extremes.\n\nExperimental work then turned to the evaluators rather than the evaluated. A 2012 PNAS study by Moss-Racusin and colleagues found that science faculty rated a male applicant as more competent than an otherwise identical female applicant. Independently of those competence ratings, faculty in the Moss-Racusin study offered the male applicant a higher starting salary. The salary disparity indicates that women's underrepresentation in math-intensive fields is partly driven by sociocultural factors such as stereotyping and socialization. Because the applications differed only in the applicant's name, the gap cannot be traced to qualifications. Yet sociocultural factors are not the only candidate explanation. Women's underrepresentation in math-intensive fields is also partly driven by gendered differences in occupational preferences. On the current evidence, at least one of these mechanisms operates, perhaps both: sociocultural pressure that shapes who is judged competent, and divergent preferences that shape who seeks entry in the first place.\n\nThe sociocultural mechanism has been documented at the level of entire disciplines. For example, Leslie and colleagues reported in 2015 that fields whose practitioners emphasize innate brilliance have fewer women. This pattern suggests that beliefs about who possesses natural talent are part of a field's culture and can deter entrants, independent of measured performance. The preference-driven component of the gap has a different source. It is a consequence of the conditions that Ceci and Williams described when they concluded in 2011 that sex discrimination in hiring, grant funding, and publishing is no longer the primary cause of women's underrepresentation in math-intensive fields. Taken together, the research that followed the 2005 controversy did not vindicate the aptitude hypothesis in its strong form. Instead, it replaced a single-cause dispute with a layered account. Average ability shows parity, evaluators display measurable bias, disciplinary cultures valorize brilliance in ways that discourage women, and preferences formed before careers begin shape the composition of these fields.",
